<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macos-27/">macOS Golden Gate</a> 27.2 adds code referencing new touch-specific gestures in apps like Safari, Notes, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/photos/">Photos</a>, and Quick Look, a possible sign Apple is preparing to release the rumored <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> touchscreen MacBook, possibly called <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">the "MacBook Ultra."</a><br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/11/m5-macbook-pro-cyber.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1028591" /><br/>
Code in the latest beta adds new gesture recognition tools to several Apple apps. Safari gets a touch sentinel recognizer, &zwnj;Photos&zwnj; has PXGTouchDwellGestureRecognizer, Notes has ICTouchPressGestureRecognizer, and Quick Look gets a dragTouch feature.<br/>
<br/>
While the code could be for the touchscreen MacBook, it's also applicable to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/sidecar/">Sidecar</a>. &zwnj;macOS Golden Gate&zwnj; brought full touch input to &zwnj;Sidecar&zwnj;, which is the feature that lets an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> be used as a display for a Mac. It's possible Apple is expanding on those &zwnj;Sidecar&zwnj; features, so it's not a clear indication of a new Mac.<br/>
<br/>
Touch-friendly changes started with the launch version of &zwnj;macOS Golden Gate&zwnj;, which added pull-to-refresh gestures in Safari, Mail, and Calendar.<br/>
<br/>
